Actually, my thought was to turn the stone into a flint stone, in which
case dropping it and picking it back up wouldn't change it.  This would
be much easier to code than having !oEnlightenment lighten the stone
for a while, and would make players less likely to say, "Hey, that gave
me a lighter loadstone; maybe I can get lighter DSM!"  (Incidentally,
what happens when you dip a loadstone into a !oPolymorph?)

For YANI justification, I agree with John H.  Loadstones are
magical--they couldn't auto-curse otherwise.  Remove curse doesn't
change the nature of that magic, while this YANI would.  There are gray
stones that are lighter than loadstones, and, of the three, flint is
the most useless, so I figured that being able to turn a loadstone into
a flint stone would not have any game-balance issues (it's a lot easier
to get a bunch of rocks than a loadstone and a !oEnlightenment).  There
are no other items which could be argued to be "magically heavy", so I
don't think there's a precedent.  This would give players another
option for dealing with loadstones (personally, I've only rarely seen
them except for the guaranteed ones at mine's end) and reward a player
for trying a creative solution (something NH tends to encourage).  The
only real argument against that I could see is that it gives the player
a chance to identify a potion and two of the four gray stones, but
there's already precedent for that (amethysts into !booze, for
instance), and anyone who's willing to pick up a gray stone in the
hopes of ID'ing all three of these is, well, yeah.  And, of course,
it's a fun wordplay.  :-)

